// LLVM Bitcode - Hello World! :: RAiNBOW_Hello-World
//   colour     : not assigned by Linguist
//   layer      : full (byte-balanced rainbow bar)
//   upstream   : https://github.com/quxianp/RAiNBOW_Hello-World
//   reference  : see https://esolangs.org/ and https://rosettacode.org/

// LLVM bitcode, in the wrapper form llvm-as produces by default: the
// two bytes 0B 17, the bitstream's own BC C0 DE magic at offset two,
// four offsets, and a version word.
//
// The detail that matters is that the four offsets are byte
// positions *relative to the version word*, so a hand-built wrapper
// points somewhere useless and still looks plausible. Reading the
// BC magic at offset four rather than two is right by exactly two
// bytes and fails on every real file.
//
// Hello World! is the bitcode block the offsets point at.
//
// llvm_bitcode: 38 bytes, dumped sixteen at a time.
//
// The bytes below are not hand-written. They are built from the
// format's own field widths by scripts/build_object_formats.py, and
// scripts/verify_encodings.py reads the fields back by name -- the
// magic number, the lengths, the offsets. A hex dump written from
// memory is wrong in one byte and looks exactly like a correct one.
//

// 0B 17 42 43 C0 DE 00 00 0C 00 00 00 0C 00 00 00 |..BC............|   <- 0000
// 1A 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 48 65 6C 6C 6F 20 |..........Hello |   <- 0010
// 57 6F 72 6C 64 21                               |World!|   <- 0020
